#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133615 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c18e198-9ea9-57dc-b9af-8c77f0da27cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field in Two Dimensions"}] :question/id #uuid "78e7cbb2-d207-5d5e-bf46-5c3b2686933f" :question/math-academy-id "e-8740" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=\\ln(2x+y)\\,\\mathbf{i}+ x^2e^{3y}\\,\\mathbf{j}$.\nEvaluate $\\nabla \\cdot\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(1,0)$." :question/worked-solution "If\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}=P\\,\\mathbf{i}+Q\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nis a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf{F}=\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F=\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇ \\cdot F &= \\frac{∂P}{∂x} + \\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\ln (2x + y)) + \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2}e^{3y}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{2x + y} + 3x^{2}e^{3y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf {F}$ at the point $(1,0)$, and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∇ \\cdot F)(1, 0) &= (2)/(2(1) + 0) + 3(1)^{2}e^{3(0)} \\\\\n&= 1 + 3 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133619 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f47e201-105a-579a-8cde-fcb6bf8cfa2a" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Divergence"}] :question/id #uuid "ac6e7a2c-19a3-595a-ba46-f38c653a9bec" :question/math-academy-id "e-8927" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $\\mathbf F$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2131/Source/Images/b5bfdb81e347992660d868824f19dfd3.png)\n\n1. $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = 0$\n2. $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F > 0$\n3. $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F < 0$\n4. $\\mathbf F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\, \\mathbf F = \\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F\n$$\n\ndenotes the divergence of the vector field $\\mathbf F$.\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $\\mathbf F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P$:\n\n- If $\\mathbf F$ acts like a source at $P$, then $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F > 0$ at $P$.\n- If $\\mathbf F$ acts like a sink at $P$, then $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F < 0$ at $P$.\n- If $\\mathbf F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P$, then $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F =0$ at $P$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2131/Source/Images/a3948ed745c39ad63bafd542288743db.png)\n\nIf\n\n$$\n\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = 0\n$$\n\neverywhere in the domain of $\\mathbf F$, we say that $\\mathbf F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statements II and III are false. For any point $P$, the sum of the inward-pointing vectors equals the sum of the outward-pointing vectors. Therefore, $\\mathbf F$ acts neither like a source nor a sink.\n- Statement IV is true. Since $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = 0$ everywhere, the vector field $\\mathbf F$ is solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133628 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6b79409-5a5b-533b-8d46-4e0e6d963b3a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field in Three Dimensions"}] :question/id #uuid "3cbc5b4a-dde4-59b0-bc34-08c4d8776e0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9489" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=(2x-y)^2\\,\\mathbf{i}+(3y-2z)^2\\,\\mathbf{j}+(x-2z)^2\\,\\mathbf{k}$. Evaluate $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(1,2,1)$." :question/worked-solution "If\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}=P\\,\\mathbf{i}+Q\\,\\mathbf{j} + R\\,\\mathbf k\n$$\n\nis a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = \\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf{F}=\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}+\\dfrac{\\partial R}{\\partial z}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇ \\cdot F &= \\frac{∂P}{∂x} + \\frac{∂Q}{∂y} + \\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂x}((2x - y)^{2}) + \\frac{∂}{∂y}((3y - 2z)^{2}) + \\frac{∂}{∂z}((x - 2z)^{2}) \\\\\n&= 4(2x - y) + 6(3y - 2z) - 4(x - 2z) \\\\\n&= 4x + 14y - 4z\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf {F}$ at the point $(1,2,1)$, and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndivF(1, 2, 1) &= 4(1) + 14(2) - 4(1) \\\\\n&= 28\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134293 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c18e198-9ea9-57dc-b9af-8c77f0da27cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field in Two Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92fa4723-5b3e-5065-a702-62444dbcbdc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x^{2} + 4y^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132201} {:answer/id #uuid "b226a090-2f16-5b50-83d1-13f3e74c9b75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4xyi + 8xyj" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132202} {:answer/id #uuid "bffe5f51-1346-578f-91b4-ce720a0224a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132203} {:answer/id #uuid "0cf0e842-d7d3-5af9-8d83-c3ca6d96696c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x^{2} + 2y^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132204} {:answer/id #uuid "276e6023-7047-520c-a500-029959a16dcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y^{2}i + 4x^{2}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cf0e842-d7d3-5af9-8d83-c3ca6d96696c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x^{2} + 2y^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132204} :answer-field/id #uuid "81d989ae-af6a-5453-99cd-3ae1697968dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132200}] :question/id #uuid "a8381fb3-8431-5be5-b51b-a5d43ddf8c5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-41817" :question/problem "Given that $F = 2xy^{2}i + 4x^{2}yj$, what is $divF$?"}]}
